      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Pain management is an important component in the postoperative period following discectomy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIMS",
          "content": "We hypothesized that mesalamine considering its better safety profile, is likely to be a better choice, if it would be as effective as ibuprofen in controlling post-discectomy pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTINGS AND DESIGN",
          "content": "A double-blind randomized controlled trial was performed on patients who underwent lumbar discectomy surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Of the 58 patients who had lumbar discectomy, 27 patients were randomized to oral ibuprofen 500 mg and 31 patients to mesalamine 400 mg, three times a day for nine days following surgery. There was no placebo group. Severity of pain was assessed by using 10- cm visual analogue scale (VAS), once before operation and for nine days after."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STATISTICAL ANALYSIS",
          "content": "Mean +/- SD pain scores were compared between groups and the statistical difference was estimated by Student's test using SPSS (Version 13). We also calculated the power of each t-test. Repeated measure ANOVA was performed for measuring the effect of time."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The age range of the patients was 35 to 60 years (mean: 42.2 years). Mean +/- SD preoperative pain scores for ibuprofen or mesalamine-treated groups were 7.852 +/- 2.441 and 7.806 +/- 2.892, respectively. At the end of day 9, mean +/- SD of pain score was 2.704 +/- 2.284 and 2.717 +/- 2.273 for ibuprofen and mesalamine-treated groups respectively. Both drugs significantly reduced postoperative pain and there was no statistically significant difference between the two groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Since both drugs showed almost equal analgesic effect, considering its safety profile mesalamine, seems to be the preferred choice to alleviate post-discectomy surgery pain."
        }
      ]},
